warning: examples/238_pcall_receive_warnings.gem:10: `pcall(f(x))` runs f(x) before `pcall` is called, so an error raised in f(x) is not caught; `pcall` catches errors only of the fn f(x) returns. Hint: to catch errors of the call itself, write `pcall f(x)` (no parentheses), or `pcall do ... end` for several statements.
warning: examples/238_pcall_receive_warnings.gem:12: `pcall(f(x))` runs f(x) before `pcall` is called, so an error raised in f(x) is not caught; `pcall` catches errors only of the fn f(x) returns. Hint: to catch errors of the call itself, write `pcall f(x)` (no parentheses), or `pcall do ... end` for several statements.
warning: examples/238_pcall_receive_warnings.gem:28: `sum_next` waits for messages (`receive`) and calls itself here before it is done, so each call keeps its stack frame and memory until the call returns; as a process loop, the stack grows with every message handled. Hint: for a loop, make the call the last thing the fn does (the last expression of its branch, or `return sum_next(...)`), or loop with `while`.
